Your application metrics are scraped from:

easytrade-demo-service

With labels like:

cluster="application-eks"
namespace="easytrade"
service="easytrade-demo-service"
job="easytrade-demo-service"
First: Find available EasyTrade metrics

In Grafana → Explore → Mimir datasource, run:



This will show the actual metrics exposed by your application.

Recommended Application Dashboard Panels
1. Application Up Status
up{
  job="easytrade-demo-service"
}

Visualization:

Stat

Expected:

1 = UP 🟢
0 = DOWN 🔴


{job="easytrade-demo-service"}

Or to see metric names:

count by (__name__) (
  {job="easytrade-demo-service"}
)


2. Application Target Count
count(
  up{
    job="easytrade-demo-service"
  }
)

Shows how many application pods Alloy is scraping.

Visualization:

Stat
3. Request Rate

If your application exposes:

http_requests_total

Use:

sum(
  rate(http_requests_total{
    job="easytrade-demo-service"
  }[5m])
)

Visualization:

Time series
4. Requests Per Second by Pod
sum by (pod) (
  rate(http_requests_total{
    job="easytrade-demo-service"
  }[5m])
)

Useful for checking traffic distribution.

5. HTTP Error Rate

If your application has HTTP status labels:

sum(
  rate(http_requests_total{
    job="easytrade-demo-service",
    status=~"5.."
  }[5m])
)
6. HTTP Success Rate
sum(
  rate(http_requests_total{
    job="easytrade-demo-service",
    status=~"2.."
  }[5m])
)
7. Application Metrics Availability
count by (__name__) (
  {job="easytrade-demo-service"}
)

This is useful initially to understand exactly what metrics your application exposes.

2️⃣ Kubernetes Cluster Dashboard

This will be one of the most important dashboards for your observability demo.

Your cluster label is:

cluster="application-eks"
🖥️ NODE METRICS
1. Number of Nodes
count(
  kubelet_node_name{
    cluster="application-eks"
  }
)


2. Node CPU Usage
100 -
(
  avg by (node) (
    rate(
      node_cpu_seconds_total{
        cluster="application-eks",
        mode="idle"
      }[5m]
    )
  ) * 100
)


3. Node memory Usage

100 *
(
  1 -
  (
    node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes{
      cluster="application-eks"
    }
    /
    node_memory_MemTotal_bytes{
      cluster="application-eks"
    }
  )
)


Visualization:

Time series
📦 POD METRICS
Running Pods:
===========
sum(kubelet_running_pods)          # running pods

Pod CPU Usage
============
sum by (namespace, pod) (
  rate(
    container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{
      cluster="application-eks",
      container!="",
      image!=""
    }[5m]
  )
)
